1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,779
What did a 500 note turn into after 8 PM?

2
00:00:03,779 --> 00:00:07,214
Just a piece of paper. On the night of 8 November 2016,

3
00:00:07,214 --> 00:00:08,589
an announcement came on TV.

4
00:00:08,589 --> 00:00:12,367
From midnight, old notes of 500 and above would stop being valid.

5
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:14,429
The money kept in the drawer at home

6
00:00:14,429 --> 00:00:16,490
suddenly became a question mark.

7
00:00:16,490 --> 00:00:17,799
Think of an ordinary home.

8
00:00:17,799 --> 00:00:21,399
Mummy had hidden some notes in the kitchen containers.

9
00:00:21,399 --> 00:00:24,671
Papa had cash in his cupboard saved for a wedding.

10
00:00:24,671 --> 00:00:26,962
The two looked at each other.

11
00:00:26,962 --> 00:00:29,580
Nobody understood what to do now.

12
00:00:29,580 --> 00:00:32,875
This was no small matter. According to the RBI,

13
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:37,634
about 86 percent of the value of notes in the market that night

14
00:00:37,634 --> 00:00:41,661
stopped being legal notes all at once. Legal notes, meaning legal tender,

15
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:44,590
which nobody can refuse to accept.

16
00:00:44,590 --> 00:00:48,500
It meant most of the money in the market stopped in one night.

17
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,633
The milkman, the vegetable seller, the auto driver,

18
00:00:50,633 --> 00:00:54,187
everyone had those same big notes in hand. And the next morning,

19
00:00:54,187 --> 00:00:56,320
everyone still had to spend money.

20
00:00:56,320 --> 00:00:59,136
Today we will hear this whole story. The first question:

21
00:00:59,136 --> 00:01:03,158
what did ordinary families go through in those months of queues?

22
00:01:03,158 --> 00:01:05,974
The second question: when the RBI counted the notes,

23
00:01:05,974 --> 00:01:08,790
how much money came back? The third question:

24
00:01:08,790 --> 00:01:12,410
how did this shock change our money habits?

25
00:01:12,410 --> 00:01:15,634
Let's start with the first question. The next morning,

26
00:01:15,634 --> 00:01:18,500
there was a line outside every bank in the city.

27
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:22,083
The line was so long it reached the turn of the lane.

28
00:01:22,083 --> 00:01:24,590
People left their breakfast, put on their slippers,

29
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:26,740
and just stood in line.

30
00:01:26,740 --> 00:01:29,778
Papa went too. A bag of old notes in his hand,

31
00:01:29,778 --> 00:01:33,154
and a file with a photocopy of his ID. He thought

32
00:01:33,154 --> 00:01:36,867
it would be done in an hour. When he came home in the evening,

33
00:01:36,867 --> 00:01:39,230
even then the work was only half done.

34
00:01:39,230 --> 00:01:42,801
Inside the bank, BANK UNCLE was in bad shape too.

35
00:01:42,801 --> 00:01:45,658
Every person had questions, every person was in a hurry.

36
00:01:45,658 --> 00:01:48,158
There was a limit on withdrawing and exchanging money.

37
00:01:48,158 --> 00:01:51,014
That means you could not take out

38
00:01:51,014 --> 00:01:52,800
all of your own money, whenever you wanted.

39
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,402
The story of ATMs was even stranger. Many ATMs were empty, many were shut.

40
00:01:56,402 --> 00:01:59,676
Wherever cash came, the news spread like fire.

41
00:01:59,676 --> 00:02:03,605
People were even calling each other at night to tell each other,

42
00:02:03,605 --> 00:02:05,570
that the ATM over there has cash.

43
00:02:05,570 --> 00:02:08,637
RBI's job had now become a race.

44
00:02:08,637 --> 00:02:12,045
Printing new notes in the press, sending them to the currency chests,

45
00:02:12,045 --> 00:02:15,794
then on to banks and ATMs. A currency chest means those big vaults,

46
00:02:15,794 --> 00:02:18,180
where the banks' cash is kept.

47
00:02:18,180 --> 00:02:19,797
There was tension at home too.

48
00:02:19,797 --> 00:02:22,708
The notes from Mummy's tin box also had to be deposited.

49
00:02:22,708 --> 00:02:24,972
They were afraid the bank people would ask,

50
00:02:24,972 --> 00:02:26,589
where did so much money come from.

51
00:02:26,589 --> 00:02:28,853
Big deposits could be questioned,

52
00:02:28,853 --> 00:02:30,470
everyone had heard this.

53
00:02:30,470 --> 00:02:32,362
Here is something to understand.

54
00:02:32,362 --> 00:02:34,568
Mummy's money was not stolen.

55
00:02:34,568 --> 00:02:38,036
It was small savings of many years, saved from the vegetable money.

56
00:02:38,036 --> 00:02:40,243
But cash has no record,

57
00:02:40,243 --> 00:02:43,080
so even honest savings looked suspicious at that time.

58
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:45,337
Now let's go to the neighbourhood grocery shop.

59
00:02:45,337 --> 00:02:48,238
The SHOPKEEPER's shop ran on credit and cash.

60
00:02:48,238 --> 00:02:50,817
Every day's accounts were in a thick diary.

61
00:02:50,817 --> 00:02:52,751
In the first week after the notes were banned,

62
00:02:52,751 --> 00:02:55,330
the loose change in the cash drawer almost ran out.

63
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:59,257
Customers would come and show an old 500 note. The shopkeeper would shake his head,

64
00:02:59,257 --> 00:03:03,184
saying, brother, this won't work. Some people brought a new big note,

65
00:03:03,184 --> 00:03:07,504
but there was no change for it. The goods were in the shop, the customer was there too,

66
00:03:07,504 --> 00:03:09,860
but the transaction got stuck in the middle.

67
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:11,531
So what did the shopkeeper do?

68
00:03:11,531 --> 00:03:15,208
The same thing that always happens in our country. He opened the diary.

69
00:03:15,208 --> 00:03:18,550
He started giving credit to people he knew. He thought,

70
00:03:18,550 --> 00:03:21,225
the relationship will stay, the money will come later.

71
00:03:21,225 --> 00:03:23,230
At that time this even seemed right.

72
00:03:23,230 --> 00:03:24,666
But credit means,

73
00:03:24,666 --> 00:03:27,251
the shopkeeper himself is giving goods without getting money.

74
00:03:27,251 --> 00:03:29,549
He still has to pay cash to the wholesaler.

75
00:03:29,549 --> 00:03:32,135
So the pressure in between fell on his own pocket.

76
00:03:32,135 --> 00:03:34,720
For a small shop, this was a blow lasting months.

77
00:03:34,720 --> 00:03:36,947
Right at this time, one more thing happened.

78
00:03:36,947 --> 00:03:39,917
On some shops a paper started appearing stuck up,

79
00:03:39,917 --> 00:03:41,774
with a QR code on it.

80
00:03:41,774 --> 00:03:45,857
For the first time, many shopkeepers started taking money through the phone.

81
00:03:45,857 --> 00:03:49,570
In the beginning, even he was scared. Would the money really come?

82
00:03:49,570 --> 00:03:51,869
The brother explained to the shopkeeper, Uncle,

83
00:03:51,869 --> 00:03:54,934
the money on the phone goes straight into the bank.

84
00:03:54,934 --> 00:03:58,765
No more hassle of change. The shopkeeper quietly said yes.

85
00:03:58,765 --> 00:04:02,980
The first payment came, and a slight smile appeared on his face.

86
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:06,053
But it wasn't easy for everyone. In villages,

87
00:04:06,053 --> 00:04:09,808
or for the elderly, a smartphone and a bank app were something new.

88
00:04:09,808 --> 00:04:12,880
The people who took their daily earnings in cash,

89
00:04:12,880 --> 00:04:15,270
for them these months were the hardest.

90
00:04:15,270 --> 00:04:17,487
Now the second question, the biggest question.

91
00:04:17,487 --> 00:04:20,443
When all the notes came back to the bank,

92
00:04:20,443 --> 00:04:23,768
the RBI counted them. This counting was not easy.

93
00:04:23,768 --> 00:04:27,463
A huge pile of notes was checked one by one in machines.

94
00:04:27,463 --> 00:04:29,680
Not fake, but genuine, right?

95
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:31,751
In the beginning, many people thought

96
00:04:31,751 --> 00:04:33,526
that a big part would not come back.

97
00:04:33,526 --> 00:04:36,484
The thinking was that those who have hidden money

98
00:04:36,484 --> 00:04:37,963
would be afraid to come to the bank.

99
00:04:37,963 --> 00:04:40,330
And those notes would just end right there.

100
00:04:40,330 --> 00:04:44,029
Then the RBI's final account came. Of the notes that were banned,

101
00:04:44,029 --> 00:04:47,056
almost all came back to the bank.

102
00:04:47,056 --> 00:04:49,410
Meaning almost all the money returned.

103
00:04:49,410 --> 00:04:52,100
Only a very small part stayed outside.

104
00:04:52,100 --> 00:04:53,788
What does this directly mean?

105
00:04:53,788 --> 00:04:57,164
No big hidden pile was found in the form of notes.

106
00:04:57,164 --> 00:05:00,877
People somehow brought their money to the bank.

107
00:05:00,877 --> 00:05:04,590
The first thinking was shown a different picture by the final number.

108
00:05:04,590 --> 00:05:07,106
But this return had one more effect

109
00:05:07,106 --> 00:05:08,679
that few people know about.

110
00:05:08,679 --> 00:05:11,195
So much money came into the bank all at once,

111
00:05:11,195 --> 00:05:13,082
into savings and current accounts.

112
00:05:13,082 --> 00:05:15,284
For the bank, this was cheap money,

113
00:05:15,284 --> 00:05:17,800
because it has to pay less interest on it.

114
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:20,002
When the bank has cheap money,

115
00:05:20,002 --> 00:05:23,461
it can give cheaper loans too. According to the RBI,

116
00:05:23,461 --> 00:05:28,494
banks cut their interest on one-year loans by 100 basis points.

117
00:05:28,494 --> 00:05:31,010
A basis point is a small unit for measuring interest.

118
00:05:31,010 --> 00:05:31,995
Who benefits from this?

119
00:05:31,995 --> 00:05:34,952
Those who had a home loan or car loan running.

120
00:05:34,952 --> 00:05:37,579
With the interest going down, the EMI could become lighter.

121
00:05:37,579 --> 00:05:39,878
That means the family that stood in the queue,

122
00:05:39,878 --> 00:05:42,835
was the one who got a little relief on their EMI later.

123
00:05:42,835 --> 00:05:43,820
Strange, isn't it?

124
00:05:43,820 --> 00:05:47,304
Now the third question. How did this shock change our habits?

125
00:05:47,304 --> 00:05:49,394
The answer is in your pocket today.

126
00:05:49,394 --> 00:05:52,181
Be it a tea stall or a vegetable cart,

127
00:05:52,181 --> 00:05:53,923
that QR code is everywhere.

128
00:05:53,923 --> 00:05:56,710
But this change didn't happen in a single day.

129
00:05:56,710 --> 00:05:59,364
According to a Business Standard report,

130
00:05:59,364 --> 00:06:02,018
according to NPCI data,

131
00:06:02,018 --> 00:06:07,327
in FY17, UPI saw only 17.8 million transactions in the whole year.

132
00:06:07,327 --> 00:06:10,866
NPCI is the organization that runs UPI.

133
00:06:10,866 --> 00:06:13,520
FY means the government's accounting year.

134
00:06:13,520 --> 00:06:18,311
And by FY26? This number reached 241.62 billion transactions.

135
00:06:18,311 --> 00:06:20,489
That is a growth of thousands of times.

136
00:06:20,489 --> 00:06:24,410
That means something only a handful of people used,

137
00:06:24,410 --> 00:06:28,330
is now a daily habit in every home.

138
00:06:28,330 --> 00:06:30,517
But the report says one more thing.

139
00:06:30,517 --> 00:06:33,329
Cash hasn't disappeared completely. According to the report,

140
00:06:33,329 --> 00:06:37,078
India's cash habit survived even during the UPI boom.

141
00:06:37,078 --> 00:06:38,953
People pay with their phones,

142
00:06:38,953 --> 00:06:41,140
and also keep cash in the cupboard.

143
00:06:41,140 --> 00:06:44,586
Now, the mistake many people make. Some think,

144
00:06:44,586 --> 00:06:47,405
everything is UPI now, there's no need for cash at all.

145
00:06:47,405 --> 00:06:50,538
Some still think cash is the only real money,

146
00:06:50,538 --> 00:06:53,670
and they don't trust banks. Both ways of thinking are the same mistake.

147
00:06:53,670 --> 00:06:57,262
Why? Because 2016 showed that keeping only cash is risky.

148
00:06:57,262 --> 00:06:59,875
Overnight, your cash can get stuck.

149
00:06:59,875 --> 00:07:03,141
And relying only on digital is risky too. If the network goes,

150
00:07:03,141 --> 00:07:06,080
the server is down, the battery dies, then you're stuck too.

151
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:09,834
Now listen to the WIFE, who runs the household budget.

152
00:07:09,834 --> 00:07:12,223
Since 2016, she made a simple rule.

153
00:07:12,223 --> 00:07:13,929
Daily expenses by phone.

154
00:07:13,929 --> 00:07:16,318
But always keep a little cash at home,

155
00:07:16,318 --> 00:07:19,730
just enough to cover a few days of essential expenses.

156
00:07:19,730 --> 00:07:23,503
And the money for big savings? Not in a box, but in the bank.

157
00:07:23,503 --> 00:07:27,275
Because money in the bank is on record, and you get interest too.

158
00:07:27,275 --> 00:07:29,333
And if any rule changes tomorrow,

159
00:07:29,333 --> 00:07:32,420
you have proof that this money is yours.

160
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:34,226
This is called an emergency cushion.

161
00:07:34,226 --> 00:07:37,839
An emergency cushion means money set aside for hard times.

162
00:07:37,839 --> 00:07:41,813
Keep most of it in your bank account, where you can withdraw it right away.

163
00:07:41,813 --> 00:07:43,981
And keep a small part as cash in hand,

164
00:07:43,981 --> 00:07:46,510
for when the bank or the network doesn't help you.

165
00:07:46,510 --> 00:07:48,174
One more lesson, from the shopkeeper.

166
00:07:48,174 --> 00:07:50,835
A shop that accepts both cash and QR,

167
00:07:50,835 --> 00:07:52,831
its customers don't go back empty-handed.

168
00:07:52,831 --> 00:07:55,493
The same rule applies to your home too.

169
00:07:55,493 --> 00:07:58,488
If one way is shut, another should stay open.

170
00:07:58,488 --> 00:08:00,151
This is not investment advice,

171
00:08:00,151 --> 00:08:02,480
just a sensible way to run your household.

172
00:08:02,480 --> 00:08:04,750
So here is the full account of the story.

173
00:08:04,750 --> 00:08:10,427
On 8 November 2016, about 86 percent of the value of notes was shut down overnight.

174
00:08:10,427 --> 00:08:13,832
Lines at the bank, credit at the shop, fear at home.

175
00:08:13,832 --> 00:08:16,103
Then almost all the notes came back,

176
00:08:16,103 --> 00:08:19,130
and paying by phone became a habit.

177
00:08:19,130 --> 00:08:20,929
In the next video, another story,

178
00:08:20,929 --> 00:08:23,627
one that is directly connected to your money. Till then,

179
00:08:23,627 --> 00:08:27,523
check the balance of cash and digital at your home once.

180
00:08:27,523 --> 00:08:29,322
And send this video to Papa,

181
00:08:29,322 --> 00:08:32,020
who even today keeps all his cash in the cupboard.

182
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:35,382
Now a simple question for you.

183
00:08:35,382 --> 00:08:42,779
During the note ban, what was hardest at your home,

184
00:08:42,779 --> 00:08:48,158
the bank line, the ATM, or getting change at the shop?

185
00:08:48,158 --> 00:08:51,520
Write your story in the comments.
